 ## Overview
-This method extracts isosurfaces from unstructured 3D scalar fields defined on tetrahedral meshes by streaming cells directly into graphics hardware, computing triangles during rendering time without storing them explicitly [S1]. It sets itself apart from CPU-based extraction by leveraging vertex shaders for immediate feedback on isovalue changes and from brute-force GPU methods by optionally using a hybrid CPU/GPU interval tree to pre-select intersecting cells, achieving up to 7x speedup over brute-force GPU rendering [S1]. Unlike structured grid methods (e.g., [Marching Cubes](52-marching-cubes.md)), it handles arbitrary tetrahedral connectivity by treating each cell independently in the vertex pipeline [S2, book].
+This method extracts isosurfaces from unstructured 3D scalar fields defined on tetrahedral meshes by streaming cells directly into graphics hardware, computing triangles during rendering time without storing them explicitly [S1]. It sets itself apart from CPU-based extraction by leveraging vertex shaders for immediate feedback on isovalue changes, and from brute-force GPU methods by optionally using a hybrid CPU/GPU interval tree to pre-select intersecting cells, achieving up to 7x speedup over brute-force GPU rendering [S1]. Unlike structured grid methods (e.g., [Marching Cubes](52-marching-cubes.md)), it handles arbitrary tetrahedral connectivity by processing each cell's vertices through a vertex program that classifies them relative to the isovalue and outputs intersection points directly [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of tetrahedra. Each tetrahedron is defined by 4 vertices. For each vertex, the input data includes: 3D position ($\mathbf{p}$), normal vector ($\mathbf{n}$), scalar value ($s$), and a unique identifier $i \in \{1, 2, 3, 4\}$ indicating the vertex's role in the current tetrahedron processing batch. The global isovalue ($isoValue$) is a uniform parameter.
-*   **Output:** A stream of vertices forming triangle strips (length 4) that approximate the isosurface. The rasterizer discards degenerate triangles (zero area) generated for non-intersecting or single-triangle cases.
